# Boundaries: number of keys and text length

Every call, in both modes, for every measured key count and text length. Use it to look up a case close to yours.
[README.md](README.md) has the summary, [METHOD.md](METHOD.md) the measurement.

- **Prepared, 32 keys or more**: faster everywhere ({{PREPARED_32_PLUS}} on random keys, {{PREPARED_NATURAL_32_PLUS}} on PHP names), except a match in the first 64 bytes: {{START_32_PLUS_LO}} to {{START_32_PLUS_HI}}.
- **Prepared, 16 keys**: {{PREPARED_16}}. **8 keys**: {{PREPARED_8_ALL}} on random keys; GCC and Clang often disagree on the sign.
- **One-shot**: slower on short texts, faster on long ones. Where it starts to pay off: [README.md](README.md#when-a-single-call-pays-for-the-build).

{{GRID_LEGEND}}

## Random keys

Keys are 4 to 16 random letters a–p that never occur in the corpus, so the matches are only where we put them.
Texts are windows of English PHP documentation at random positions.
Each table title says where the matches are put:

- **one occurrence in the middle third**, **in the first 64 bytes** or **in the last 64 bytes**: one key at a random position in that region;
- **no occurrence**: the text has no key;
- **16 occurrences across the text**: the text is cut into 16 equal parts with one key in each (with 8 keys, each key twice). Measured from 1 KiB.

